WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.747
WHERE is the most familiar location for subqueries.

00:00:03.747 --> 00:00:09.287
The inner result can become a benchmark, a membership set, an existence condition, or a set comparison.

00:00:09.287 --> 00:00:14.947
The final job is always Boolean: should the current outer row remain?

00:00:14.947 --> 00:00:18.767
That is why many subquery patterns appear naturally in WHERE.

00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:24.427
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:24.427 --> 00:00:30.477
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:30.477 --> 00:00:32.322
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:32.322 --> 00:00:37.337
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product,

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:44.552
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:44.552 --> 00:00:48.372
before discussing syntax.

00:00:48.372 --> 00:00:53.760
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
